\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Cross-domain check on \textsc  {TL;DR} summarisation, \emph  {superseded}. This is an archived reporting table and its surpluses are measured on 100 prompts that the audit of Appendix~\ref {app:overlap} found to lie entirely inside this panel's training set, so they are in-sample fits and the description of them as held out, which this caption previously carried, was wrong. The audited replacement is Table~\ref {tab:objectivewise-appendix}; the numbers below are retained only as the record of what was originally reported. Population scale of \eqref  {eq:centered}, judged by the same pinned \texttt  {Qwen3-32B} oracle and anchored on the same \texttt  {Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct} reference used throughout. Every arm starts at $\pi _0=\mu $, trains on the identical eight-response pool with the identical optimizer budget, and differs only in the weight vector over objectives, so the comparison isolates the aggregation rule. The panel is chosen because its objectives conflict by construction---coverage and conciseness cannot both be maximised---and they do: their game gradients correlate $0.203$ against a mean of $0.399$ across all pairs, where the \textsc  {UltraFeedback} panel averages $0.616$. That conflict separates the rules here in a way it does not there: each rule now buys its favoured objective at a visible cost in another, and no arm is uniformly best. The \textsc  {SafeRLHF} panel is not yet built and is omitted rather than left blank.}}{21}{table.159}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:cross-domain-completion}{{8}{21}{Cross-domain check on \textsc {TL;DR} summarisation, \emph {superseded}. This is an archived reporting table and its surpluses are measured on 100 prompts that the audit of Appendix~\ref {app:overlap} found to lie entirely inside this panel's training set, so they are in-sample fits and the description of them as held out, which this caption previously carried, was wrong. The audited replacement is Table~\ref {tab:objectivewise-appendix}; the numbers below are retained only as the record of what was originally reported. Population scale of \eqref {eq:centered}, judged by the same pinned \texttt {Qwen3-32B} oracle and anchored on the same \texttt {Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct} reference used throughout. Every arm starts at $\pi _0=\mu $, trains on the identical eight-response pool with the identical optimizer budget, and differs only in the weight vector over objectives, so the comparison isolates the aggregation rule. The panel is chosen because its objectives conflict by construction---coverage and conciseness cannot both be maximised---and they do: their game gradients correlate $0.203$ against a mean of $0.399$ across all pairs, where the \textsc {UltraFeedback} panel averages $0.616$. That conflict separates the rules here in a way it does not there: each rule now buys its favoured objective at a visible cost in another, and no arm is uniformly best. The \textsc {SafeRLHF} panel is not yet built and is omitted rather than left blank}{table.159}{}}
